The authentication process is handled through Keycloak’s Single Sign-On (SSO) portal using the OpenID Connect (OIDC) protocol. When a user logs in, Keycloak validates the credentials and issues a set of session tokens based on OAuth 2.0 standards—an ID token for user identity and an access token for authorization. These tokens, encoded as JWTs, are included in subsequent API requests to the Validium and Paladin services. Each backend service validates the token’s signature and claims through Keycloak’s JWKS endpoint, ensuring secure, centralized authentication and authorization across all components in the shared environment.